WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.712 --> 00:00:03.223
- I can't watch myself on screen.

00:00:03.223 --> 00:00:05.408
Then why the fuck should we
pay 12.50 to watch your ass

00:00:05.408 --> 00:00:06.802
if you're not going to?

00:00:06.802 --> 00:00:08.748
All the getting high,
the drinking, the drugs,

00:00:08.748 --> 00:00:10.149
and everything else I did,

00:00:10.149 --> 00:00:12.130
I showed up at the theater every day.

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:13.735
And then when you go to soap auditions

00:00:13.735 --> 00:00:15.710
and that doesn't happen,
you say to yourself,

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:18.479
what I really need is one
good national beer commercial.

00:00:18.479 --> 00:00:20.453
(host laughs)
That's what I need.

00:00:20.453 --> 00:00:22.473
- He's a prolific actor and film producer

00:00:22.473 --> 00:00:25.397
whose appeared in over 100 movies.

00:00:25.397 --> 00:00:27.379
He achieved prominence in the early 1990s

00:00:27.379 --> 00:00:29.807
with films like Patriot
Games, Jurassic Park,

00:00:29.807 --> 00:00:31.330
and Pulp Fiction.

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:33.480
He's ranked as the second
highest box office star

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.745
of all time, with box office earnings

00:00:35.745 --> 00:00:38.093
of almost five billion dollars.

00:00:38.093 --> 00:00:39.740
He's Samuel L. Jackson, and here's my take

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:42.215
on his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:42.215 --> 00:00:43.654
Rule number two is my personal favorite,

00:00:43.654 --> 00:00:45.642
and make sure to stick
around all the way to the end

00:00:45.642 --> 00:00:47.290
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:48.894
And as always, if Samuel says something

00:00:48.894 --> 00:00:50.413
that really means something to you,

00:00:50.413 --> 00:00:52.312
please leave it in the comments below

00:00:52.312 --> 00:00:54.460
and put quotes around it so other people

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:56.960
can be inspired as well.
Enjoy.

00:00:59.526 --> 00:01:02.276
(newsroom music)

00:01:09.499 --> 00:01:11.730
- Listen, there's so
much shit you don't know

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:13.589
about what will happen to
you if you become an actor

00:01:13.589 --> 00:01:17.706
or you become famous, that
all this shit is gravy.

00:01:17.706 --> 00:01:19.206
That's what it is.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:21.747
- But you're still doing the work.

00:01:21.747 --> 00:01:23.560
- Yeah, it's about the work.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:25.408
It's not about all the other shit.

00:01:25.408 --> 00:01:27.074
The other shit is a byproduct of it.

00:01:27.074 --> 00:01:28.626
Now you can buy into the other shit

00:01:28.626 --> 00:01:31.802
and lose yourself in it, and lose yourself

00:01:31.802 --> 00:01:33.865
in the bullshit people
tell you about yourself

00:01:33.865 --> 00:01:36.260
and about the kind of shit you do.

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:38.397
Everything I do is not great.

00:01:38.397 --> 00:01:40.480
Good, but it ain't great.

00:01:41.658 --> 00:01:43.469
I do good work in some shit movies.

00:01:43.469 --> 00:01:44.912
I know that.
That's happened.

00:01:44.912 --> 00:01:46.685
I know what they are.
They know what they are.

00:01:46.685 --> 00:01:48.413
But people watch them anyway

00:01:48.413 --> 00:01:49.612
because there's something about them

00:01:49.612 --> 00:01:51.424
that made me want to do it.

00:01:51.424 --> 00:01:52.989
I enjoyed myself doing it.
It just didn't work out well.

00:01:52.989 --> 00:01:55.811
But if you buy into all this shit,

00:01:55.811 --> 00:01:59.879
or you start bullshitting
yourself about what all this is,

00:01:59.879 --> 00:02:02.629
then you can get fucked up in it.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:07.520
I read everything people write about me

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:09.707
because I'm that kind of guy.

00:02:09.707 --> 00:02:12.014
I remember a lot of people
that say bad shit about me

00:02:12.014 --> 00:02:13.787
because I'm that kind of guy.

00:02:13.787 --> 00:02:16.755
I remember the people who
write good shit about me

00:02:16.755 --> 00:02:18.240
because I'm that kind of guy.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:19.929
We're in this business
because it's a look at me

00:02:19.929 --> 00:02:22.520
fucking business, and if you say

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:24.742
you don't do that shit, you're a liar.

00:02:24.742 --> 00:02:25.812
You're lying to yourself,

00:02:25.812 --> 00:02:27.540
you're lying to the people who watch you.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:28.987
Even those motherfuckers that go,

00:02:28.987 --> 00:02:30.802
I can't watch myself on screen.

00:02:30.802 --> 00:02:33.847
Well why the fuck should we
pay 12.50 to watch your ass

00:02:33.847 --> 00:02:36.116
if you're not going to, ya know?

00:02:36.116 --> 00:02:40.114
If you can't watch it, I damn
sure don't want to watch it.

00:02:40.114 --> 00:02:42.705
I watch my movies.
I want to see myself.

00:02:42.705 --> 00:02:45.423
When I was on stage,
I always wanted to see

00:02:45.423 --> 00:02:48.065
the play I was in with me in it.

00:02:48.065 --> 00:02:49.879
This is the perfect venue for that.

00:02:49.879 --> 00:02:51.406
I watch my fucking movies.

00:02:51.406 --> 00:02:53.467
Some of them I watch over and over again.

00:02:53.467 --> 00:02:55.283
If I'm channel surfing
and nothing else is on

00:02:55.283 --> 00:02:57.384
and I pass a movie I'm in, I'm in.

00:02:57.384 --> 00:02:59.143
I'm there watching it.

00:02:59.143 --> 00:03:02.335
Because that's what it is.
It's a look at me business.

00:03:02.335 --> 00:03:05.716
And I got into it because I
enjoyed the fantasy of it.

00:03:05.716 --> 00:03:08.357
I love make believe.
I love movies.

00:03:08.357 --> 00:03:11.526
I love entertaining people.
That's what we do.

00:03:11.526 --> 00:03:12.922
I kept going.
I had a path.

00:03:12.922 --> 00:03:14.119
I knew what my path was.

00:03:14.119 --> 00:03:17.225
My path was to become what I am right now.

00:03:17.225 --> 00:03:20.304
Now I lost sight of it in
an interesting sort of way,

00:03:20.304 --> 00:03:23.387
or I went about it in a wayward path,

00:03:26.605 --> 00:03:30.303
but fortunately for me,
all the getting high,

00:03:30.303 --> 00:03:33.529
the drinking, the drugs,
and everything else I did,

00:03:33.529 --> 00:03:36.612
I showed up at the theater every day.

00:03:36.612 --> 00:03:39.435
I did plays ripped out of my mind.

00:03:39.435 --> 00:03:42.204
I rehearsed ripped out of my mind,

00:03:42.204 --> 00:03:44.263
but every lesson that I learned

00:03:44.263 --> 00:03:46.739
I held on to for some reason.

00:03:46.739 --> 00:03:49.959
God, whatever, somebody,
let me hold on to everything

00:03:49.959 --> 00:03:51.361
that I was learning.

00:03:51.361 --> 00:03:53.293
My stage lefts, my stage rights,

00:03:53.293 --> 00:03:56.637
my upstage foot always
out, turn down this way,

00:03:56.637 --> 00:03:59.569
learning how to develop a character,

00:03:59.569 --> 00:04:01.169
doing all those things,

00:04:01.169 --> 00:04:03.010
because those are the things that I love.

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:04.406
I did them in the midst of everything else

00:04:04.406 --> 00:04:05.629
that I was doing.

00:04:05.629 --> 00:04:08.699
When my friends were getting
ready to get some more drugs,

00:04:08.699 --> 00:04:10.550
I go oh, I got to be at the theater.

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:13.393
I'll catch you all when the
theater is over, when I'm done.

00:04:13.393 --> 00:04:14.696
And I would jet.

00:04:14.696 --> 00:04:17.233
Or I got to go build a
set, I got to do this,

00:04:17.233 --> 00:04:19.256
so I did everything that I had to do.

00:04:19.256 --> 00:04:21.940
I was focused on this
by any means necessary.

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:23.924
Plus I had a professor
who was like, you know,

00:04:23.924 --> 00:04:25.986
well all the great ones do their jobs

00:04:25.986 --> 00:04:27.472
and get ripped out of their minds.

00:04:27.472 --> 00:04:30.929
You look at the Peter O'Tooles
and the duh duh duh ...

00:04:30.929 --> 00:04:32.785
They get drunk, they get
high, they do what they do.

00:04:32.785 --> 00:04:34.721
But they show up, and they do the job.

00:04:34.721 --> 00:04:37.314
So we were like, okay,
and that's what we did.

00:04:37.314 --> 00:04:38.757
(audience laughs)

00:04:38.757 --> 00:04:41.518
We drank with him, and we went
to Styles of Acting class.

00:04:41.518 --> 00:04:43.588
We'd go smoke a joint and come in

00:04:43.588 --> 00:04:45.769
and do our other acting
classes or go build a set

00:04:45.769 --> 00:04:47.997
or build some costumes
or hang some lights.

00:04:47.997 --> 00:04:49.565
All the things that we had to do.

00:04:49.565 --> 00:04:52.575
So I kept learning my
lesson and doing them.

00:04:52.575 --> 00:04:54.302
- How often do you find
when you get on set

00:04:54.302 --> 00:04:56.403
people are like, do the Samuel,
do the Sam Jackson thing,

00:04:56.403 --> 00:04:59.531
do the Samuel L. Jackson
thing that we know and love.

00:04:59.531 --> 00:05:02.742
- Not often.
I don't know what that is.

00:05:02.742 --> 00:05:05.926
(host and audience laugh)

00:05:05.926 --> 00:05:09.139
I mean, unless it's
saying (audio goes silent)

00:05:09.139 --> 00:05:11.695
like eight times.
(audience laughs)

00:05:11.695 --> 00:05:15.870
Well crafted (mumbles).
(audience applauds)

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:19.492
I don't really know
what that is, or to me,

00:05:19.492 --> 00:05:23.296
the Sam Jackson thing
is I've read the script,

00:05:23.296 --> 00:05:26.534
you know, I don't know how many times,

00:05:26.534 --> 00:05:30.206
and I've broken my character
down the way I learned

00:05:30.206 --> 00:05:33.503
to break a character down in the theater.

00:05:33.503 --> 00:05:35.260
If there's source material

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:36.829
like for George Washington Williams,

00:05:36.829 --> 00:05:38.642
I'll go to the source
material and deal with that,

00:05:38.642 --> 00:05:40.945
but, if not, then okay,
it's up to me to figure out

00:05:40.945 --> 00:05:42.245
who this guy is.

00:05:42.245 --> 00:05:44.683
I can figure out when he was
born, who his parents were,

00:05:44.683 --> 00:05:48.846
what his educational background
is, military if he has that,

00:05:48.846 --> 00:05:52.379
what kind of life experiences he's had,

00:05:52.379 --> 00:05:54.480
how he feels about this,
that, or the other,

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:56.704
even down sometimes to
what kind of food he eats,

00:05:56.704 --> 00:05:59.550
you know, just because there
are eating scenes in the movie,

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:01.645
and I want to figure
out what I want to eat

00:06:01.645 --> 00:06:04.202
when I'm in that particular scene.

00:06:04.202 --> 00:06:06.715
And then from scene to scene,
what my relationship is

00:06:06.715 --> 00:06:09.104
with those characters or
what I'm trying to accomplish

00:06:09.104 --> 00:06:10.962
in this scene that leads to the next scene

00:06:10.962 --> 00:06:13.267
that leads to the next scene
that leads to the next scene.

00:06:13.267 --> 00:06:17.140
So what happens is, the
Sam Jackson thing, for me,

00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:21.281
according to my agents and
managers, is I'm so prepared

00:06:21.281 --> 00:06:24.874
when I show up that I
expect everybody else

00:06:24.874 --> 00:06:29.018
to be as prepared as I am,
so when a director does not

00:06:29.018 --> 00:06:33.108
have a shot list for that day,
or a director's floundering

00:06:33.108 --> 00:06:36.095
and trying to figure out what he's doing,

00:06:36.095 --> 00:06:38.520
he can ask me and I
can tell him, you know,

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:41.318
because one of the funny
things about movies

00:06:41.318 --> 00:06:44.446
is the director's usually
the person on the film set

00:06:44.446 --> 00:06:46.258
that's got the least amount of experience

00:06:46.258 --> 00:06:50.413
of everybody there.
(audience laughs)

00:06:50.413 --> 00:06:54.019
- The director makes one
movie every four years,

00:06:54.019 --> 00:06:55.407
every two years--

00:06:55.407 --> 00:06:58.740
- A good director's career is 15 movies.

00:07:00.358 --> 00:07:03.452
Everybody here's done 15
movies in the last two years.

00:07:03.452 --> 00:07:06.866
So we all know, why the
fuck is he shooting that?

00:07:06.866 --> 00:07:08.974
That ain't going to be in the movie.

00:07:08.974 --> 00:07:10.242
(audience laughs)

00:07:10.242 --> 00:07:12.525
It's like, you don't
really need that shot.

00:07:12.525 --> 00:07:14.306
You don't need that shot
(said as he coughs).

00:07:14.306 --> 00:07:17.139
(audience laughs)

00:07:19.816 --> 00:07:21.767
What did I digress from?

00:07:21.767 --> 00:07:23.356
- No, that was amazing.

00:07:23.356 --> 00:07:24.409
I don't care if you digressed.

00:07:24.409 --> 00:07:27.023
- I show up prepared, so, to me,

00:07:27.023 --> 00:07:29.562
that's the Sam Jackson thing.

00:07:29.562 --> 00:07:31.170
When you hire me, you hired an actor

00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:33.562
who going to show up, who
going to know his lines,

00:07:33.562 --> 00:07:36.440
whose going to hit his
marks, and do his best

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:39.609
to make the other actors
around him better.

00:07:39.609 --> 00:07:41.006
- [Host] Absolutely.

00:07:41.006 --> 00:07:42.489
- I'm not going to call shots for you,

00:07:42.489 --> 00:07:46.565
and I don't expect you to
give me a line reading.

00:07:46.565 --> 00:07:49.894
And if you do, you hired
the wrong motherfucker.

00:07:49.894 --> 00:07:51.793
Young actors have aspirations.

00:07:51.793 --> 00:07:53.767
You look at movies and
you look at television,

00:07:53.767 --> 00:07:57.187
and especially every year,
the Academy Awards show

00:07:57.187 --> 00:07:59.498
comes around and you
sit there and you say,

00:07:59.498 --> 00:08:01.519
God, I really would love to be there.

00:08:01.519 --> 00:08:03.619
What do I have to do to get to that point?

00:08:03.619 --> 00:08:05.383
You stand in the mirror,

00:08:05.383 --> 00:08:08.110
you do your great acceptance speech,

00:08:08.110 --> 00:08:09.839
all the things I would
say if I was up there,

00:08:09.839 --> 00:08:12.806
who I would thank, and
as you keep working,

00:08:12.806 --> 00:08:14.947
I often tell people this now,

00:08:14.947 --> 00:08:17.616
that what happened to me
was I actually kept working

00:08:17.616 --> 00:08:20.754
to the point where I
started to do better plays,

00:08:20.754 --> 00:08:23.266
and different kinds of directors

00:08:23.266 --> 00:08:25.909
that I came into contact
with and other actors

00:08:25.909 --> 00:08:28.954
and interacting with
them, and the craft became

00:08:28.954 --> 00:08:31.304
a lot more important, but there were times

00:08:31.304 --> 00:08:33.975
when I stopped wanting to be a movie star,

00:08:33.975 --> 00:08:38.014
and I said, well, okay, I
can, I can do a TV series.

00:08:38.014 --> 00:08:39.865
Sure, I can do a sitcom.

00:08:39.865 --> 00:08:41.675
And then when you
audition for a few sitcoms

00:08:41.675 --> 00:08:43.487
and you don't get that
job, you say to yourself,

00:08:43.487 --> 00:08:47.243
okay, I can do a soap, yeah,
I can sign a two-year contract

00:08:47.243 --> 00:08:49.801
and do a soap for awhile,
and then when you go

00:08:49.801 --> 00:08:51.701
to soap auditions and that doesn't happen,

00:08:51.701 --> 00:08:53.435
you say to yourself, what I really need

00:08:53.435 --> 00:08:55.577
is one good national beer commercial.

00:08:55.577 --> 00:08:56.891
That's what I need.

00:08:56.891 --> 00:08:58.497
A good national beer commercial.

00:08:58.497 --> 00:09:00.098
I can get those checks coming every week,

00:09:00.098 --> 00:09:01.785
and I can keep my theater habit going.

00:09:01.785 --> 00:09:03.231
But when all this stuff doesn't happen

00:09:03.231 --> 00:09:04.882
and you continue to work at your craft

00:09:04.882 --> 00:09:07.891
and you do theater and you
work with August Wilsons

00:09:07.891 --> 00:09:11.027
and the Lloyd Richards and
the Douglas Turner Wards

00:09:11.027 --> 00:09:13.086
of the world and the Charles Fullers,

00:09:13.086 --> 00:09:16.258
you develop a way of working,
and your craft becomes

00:09:16.258 --> 00:09:19.390
just one of those great satisfying things

00:09:19.390 --> 00:09:20.873
that you go to the theater every night,

00:09:20.873 --> 00:09:22.561
you have a different audience.

00:09:22.561 --> 00:09:24.415
People are sitting there, and you can feel

00:09:24.415 --> 00:09:27.988
the sharing of energy
between you and them.

00:09:27.988 --> 00:09:30.474
They're giving you something,
you're giving them something,

00:09:30.474 --> 00:09:32.579
and at the end every night,
there is that great reward

00:09:32.579 --> 00:09:37.030
(claps) the applause, and
you're very satisfied with that.

00:09:37.030 --> 00:09:39.579
And once you let go of all of
those dreams and aspirations

00:09:39.579 --> 00:09:41.722
and let your respect for what you do

00:09:41.722 --> 00:09:43.624
become the most important thing,

00:09:43.624 --> 00:09:45.560
the other things start to happen.

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:47.207
There was a point when I went to auditions

00:09:47.207 --> 00:09:49.764
like everybody else, no
matter what the movie was,

00:09:49.764 --> 00:09:52.525
because I wanted to be in the movies,

00:09:52.525 --> 00:09:54.048
and I wanted to go to work.

00:09:54.048 --> 00:09:55.725
And I had no choice.

00:09:55.725 --> 00:09:58.103
Going to work was the option.

00:09:58.103 --> 00:10:00.403
So generally I didn't see a script anyway.

00:10:00.403 --> 00:10:03.415
They gave you eight pages of
sides with scenes in them,

00:10:03.415 --> 00:10:05.349
and you might have got the script later.

00:10:05.349 --> 00:10:07.371
After you got the job,
they give you the script,

00:10:07.371 --> 00:10:09.261
and I used to flip through it
and see what page I died on.

00:10:09.261 --> 00:10:10.618
And that was it.

00:10:10.618 --> 00:10:14.492
But when you're out of
the audition process,

00:10:14.492 --> 00:10:16.511
and you're sitting at home
and you're making choices,

00:10:16.511 --> 00:10:18.531
then you should make choices

00:10:18.531 --> 00:10:20.556
that are satisfying to you in some way,

00:10:20.556 --> 00:10:22.135
and not monetarily.

00:10:22.135 --> 00:10:25.954
They should be satisfying
to you artistically.

00:10:25.954 --> 00:10:29.027
They should appeal to you as an actor

00:10:29.027 --> 00:10:32.670
or as an audience member or as a person

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:35.319
who wants to make people excited

00:10:35.319 --> 00:10:39.402
when they come to the
movies and see you do this.

00:10:40.958 --> 00:10:44.911
It shouldn't be based on
all that other bullshit

00:10:44.911 --> 00:10:47.655
about this is who they think I am,

00:10:47.655 --> 00:10:50.323
so I've become this kind of actor,

00:10:50.323 --> 00:10:53.089
and people appreciate me for this.

00:10:53.089 --> 00:10:54.988
That's the one thing I used
to like about Jim Carey.

00:10:54.988 --> 00:10:57.460
That everybody wanted
to go watch Jim Carey

00:10:57.460 --> 00:11:01.624
laugh and be funny, but Jim
tried all this other shit.

00:11:01.624 --> 00:11:04.424
Sometimes it worked, sometimes it didn't,

00:11:04.424 --> 00:11:08.293
but he wasn't going to be
painted into that corner,

00:11:08.293 --> 00:11:11.543
that I'm not just going to do comedies.

00:11:12.461 --> 00:11:14.730
I don't have to be the three stooges.

00:11:14.730 --> 00:11:16.737
I don't have to be W.C. Fields.

00:11:16.737 --> 00:11:18.262
I don't have to be Chaplin.

00:11:18.262 --> 00:11:20.032
I don't have to be a name.

00:11:20.032 --> 00:11:21.641
I don't have to be John Wayne.

00:11:21.641 --> 00:11:23.037
I can be all of these other things,

00:11:23.037 --> 00:11:24.560
and I want to try to be them,

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:26.457
and he made an effort to be that.

00:11:26.457 --> 00:11:28.840
When I came into this
business from the theater,

00:11:28.840 --> 00:11:30.978
I didn't do the same kind
of play all the time.

00:11:30.978 --> 00:11:32.299
I did all kinds of plays.

00:11:32.299 --> 00:11:33.618
I did all kinds of characters,

00:11:33.618 --> 00:11:35.142
and this is a bigger venue for doing that.

00:11:35.142 --> 00:11:36.538
They have better makeup artists.

00:11:36.538 --> 00:11:37.857
They have better scripts.

00:11:37.857 --> 00:11:39.095
They put you in the environment.

00:11:39.095 --> 00:11:40.577
You don't have to convince somebody.

00:11:40.577 --> 00:11:42.435
You're in the environment,
it's there, they put you in it,

00:11:42.435 --> 00:11:45.543
so why not do as many different things

00:11:45.543 --> 00:11:48.846
as you're capable of doing,
and that was my thought.

00:11:48.846 --> 00:11:52.555
I want to do as wide a
variety of genres of film

00:11:52.555 --> 00:11:55.640
as I can, just to, you
know, continue to see

00:11:55.640 --> 00:11:58.116
if I can stretch myself as an actor.

00:11:58.116 --> 00:12:00.464
But then I was in the
Die Hard office one day,

00:12:00.464 --> 00:12:03.023
and there's this sign on the wall

00:12:03.023 --> 00:12:06.523
that I adhere to vehemently now that said,

00:12:07.470 --> 00:12:09.821
be careful of the toes you step on today

00:12:09.821 --> 00:12:11.262
for they could be connected to the ass

00:12:11.262 --> 00:12:12.875
you'll have to kiss tomorrow.

00:12:12.875 --> 00:12:15.267
(host and audience laugh)

00:12:15.267 --> 00:12:19.436
And believe me, there have
been PAs and other people ...

00:12:19.436 --> 00:12:22.847
Like when I'm on a movie
set, I think everybody

00:12:22.847 --> 00:12:26.560
is just as important as I
am, or I treat them that way,

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:29.154
so I know PAs' names, I talk
to them, I speak to them

00:12:29.154 --> 00:12:31.126
every day, I speak to the
gaffers, I speak to the grips,

00:12:31.126 --> 00:12:34.341
I speak to all these
people, and over the years,

00:12:34.341 --> 00:12:38.483
I've gotten jobs, and the
producer will show up and be like,

00:12:38.483 --> 00:12:42.656
you don't remember me, but
I was a PA on so and so

00:12:42.656 --> 00:12:46.294
and so and so, you were so nice
to me that when I was doing

00:12:46.294 --> 00:12:47.824
this movie, I couldn't help but think

00:12:47.824 --> 00:12:51.649
that you would be perfect,
and that shit works out,

00:12:51.649 --> 00:12:53.836
or they become writers
or they become directors,

00:12:53.836 --> 00:12:57.006
you know, but it's a collaborative effort.

00:12:57.006 --> 00:13:01.071
We're almost the least
important element of any film.

00:13:01.071 --> 00:13:03.256
Because if they ain't
putting the lights up right,

00:13:03.256 --> 00:13:07.399
or the sound man mic is
jacked up, it don't mean shit

00:13:07.399 --> 00:13:09.714
about what you're doing
because nobody can see you

00:13:09.714 --> 00:13:13.879
or hear you, you know, so
they can fix that in the mix,

00:13:13.879 --> 00:13:16.879
but everything else has got to work,

00:13:18.149 --> 00:13:20.280
and you have to know that.

00:13:20.280 --> 00:13:23.214
I dreamed about being an
actor for a very long time,

00:13:23.214 --> 00:13:26.094
and why I was fooling myself
thinking I wanted to be

00:13:26.094 --> 00:13:28.231
an oceanographer, I'll never know.

00:13:28.231 --> 00:13:30.178
I always thought I wanted to
be the black Jacques Cousteau.

00:13:30.178 --> 00:13:32.070
I just wanted to hang out in the water.

00:13:32.070 --> 00:13:33.638
(audience laughs)

00:13:33.638 --> 00:13:35.428
I always thought inner space

00:13:35.428 --> 00:13:37.164
was always much more
exciting than outer space,

00:13:37.164 --> 00:13:38.995
so I figured I'd go
under water and hang out,

00:13:38.995 --> 00:13:41.503
because I like to dive
and do stuff like that.

00:13:41.503 --> 00:13:43.605
But I've loved movies since I was a kid.

00:13:43.605 --> 00:13:46.238
I spent a lot of my time in
the movies when I was a kid,

00:13:46.238 --> 00:13:47.593
and I watched a lot of movies.

00:13:47.593 --> 00:13:49.977
I still spend an inordinate amount

00:13:49.977 --> 00:13:51.955
of my time watching movies.

00:13:51.955 --> 00:13:54.135
I collect Asian films, so I watch a lot

00:13:54.135 --> 00:13:57.300
of Chinese, Japanese, Thai, Korean ...

00:13:57.300 --> 00:14:01.377
Koreans are making really
great films these days.

00:14:01.377 --> 00:14:04.474
Indonesian films.
I just like Asian film.

00:14:04.474 --> 00:14:08.315
It's more visual, and it
stimulates me in another way,

00:14:08.315 --> 00:14:11.864
but I grew up watching movies
and imagining myself on screen

00:14:11.864 --> 00:14:15.806
so aside from the very
serious things that I do,

00:14:15.806 --> 00:14:19.858
I will occasionally do a
Snakes on a Plane because--

00:14:19.858 --> 00:14:22.858
(audience applauds)

00:14:24.264 --> 00:14:25.837
because those are the
kind of movies I liked

00:14:25.837 --> 00:14:28.106
when I was a kid, and I
always wanted to see myself

00:14:28.106 --> 00:14:30.939
in a movie with a bunch of snakes.

00:14:32.387 --> 00:14:36.096
I'm still trying to find the
right slasher movie to do

00:14:36.096 --> 00:14:39.384
because I like slasher
movies, and I'm still,

00:14:39.384 --> 00:14:41.407
I did things like Deep Blue
Sea because I wanted to run

00:14:41.407 --> 00:14:44.371
from something that had big
teeth and all that good stuff,

00:14:44.371 --> 00:14:46.312
so I'm not that serious about it.

00:14:46.312 --> 00:14:48.873
I really think movies should be fun,

00:14:48.873 --> 00:14:51.179
and I think they should be entertaining.

00:14:51.179 --> 00:14:53.686
If I want to teach you
something, I'll do a documentary.

00:14:53.686 --> 00:14:56.530
But I do this because it feeds me.

00:14:56.530 --> 00:14:58.714
It feeds my artistic sense.

00:14:58.714 --> 00:15:01.193
A lot of people think
I do too many movies.

00:15:01.193 --> 00:15:02.554
I read an article about
me in the paper yesterday

00:15:02.554 --> 00:15:04.118
about me doing too many movies.

00:15:04.118 --> 00:15:07.175
But, you know, writers get
up and write every day.

00:15:07.175 --> 00:15:09.030
Painters get up and paint every day.

00:15:09.030 --> 00:15:10.968
Actors only have so many
acting opportunities

00:15:10.968 --> 00:15:14.180
in a life time; I'm going
to take mine as they come

00:15:14.180 --> 00:15:17.645
and do as many movies as I
can before I'm up out of here.

00:15:17.645 --> 00:15:19.048
- [Host] So on something like Avengers,

00:15:19.048 --> 00:15:21.066
it seemed like you were
having a great time.

00:15:21.066 --> 00:15:21.899
- I am.

00:15:21.899 --> 00:15:24.196
- [Host] Not a huge acting
challenge for you, I imagine.

00:15:24.196 --> 00:15:25.261
- It depends.

00:15:25.261 --> 00:15:27.078
- [Host] But you seem to be having fun.

00:15:27.078 --> 00:15:29.180
- Well, fun is important.
This should be a fun job.

00:15:29.180 --> 00:15:30.543
It's make believe.

00:15:30.543 --> 00:15:33.967
You and your friends wear
real costumes and better guns

00:15:33.967 --> 00:15:38.244
and shit doing the thing,
you know, exciting people.

00:15:38.244 --> 00:15:40.264
That's what that's about.

00:15:40.264 --> 00:15:41.872
When you're doing something
like The Avengers,

00:15:41.872 --> 00:15:43.478
you got to take it seriously

00:15:43.478 --> 00:15:47.140
in terms of you believe
the space you're in.

00:15:47.140 --> 00:15:50.350
You believe the world
and the convention of it.

00:15:50.350 --> 00:15:52.666
That there are people who can do shit

00:15:52.666 --> 00:15:54.052
greater than normal human beings can do.

00:15:54.052 --> 00:15:55.579
Trust that, go with it,

00:15:55.579 --> 00:15:57.850
treat them like they're
those kinds of people,

00:15:57.850 --> 00:16:00.611
and they have an element of
danger and an element of awe

00:16:00.611 --> 00:16:02.666
that comes with all that.

00:16:02.666 --> 00:16:05.273
That's the honesty of the world.

00:16:05.273 --> 00:16:08.116
But, you know, I'm fucking Nick Fury.

00:16:08.116 --> 00:16:11.213
I got some shit with me
too, so I can be, you know,

00:16:11.213 --> 00:16:14.466
boom, I can stand up to them
and say what I need to say,

00:16:14.466 --> 00:16:16.362
and sometimes, like in The Avengers,

00:16:16.362 --> 00:16:18.058
I need to manipulate these people.

00:16:18.058 --> 00:16:21.191
I got to where I am because
I'm Nick Fucking Fury.

00:16:21.191 --> 00:16:23.005
I can do some shit.
I got some shit with me.

00:16:23.005 --> 00:16:25.436
Nobody knows how old I
am or why I'm still alive

00:16:25.436 --> 00:16:28.813
or why I'm now black
when I used to be white,

00:16:28.813 --> 00:16:30.752
so I got some shit with me too.

00:16:30.752 --> 00:16:33.315
So you accept that and you go with it,

00:16:33.315 --> 00:16:35.292
and you treat it like
it's something honest

00:16:35.292 --> 00:16:36.979
and you treat it like it's something real,

00:16:36.979 --> 00:16:38.670
so that when people come and see it,

00:16:38.670 --> 00:16:41.476
they don't see you going (snort laughs)

00:16:41.476 --> 00:16:43.950
you know, shit I'm in it.
I'm in it.

00:16:43.950 --> 00:16:45.309
There's some dangerous shit happening,

00:16:45.309 --> 00:16:47.286
and you motherfuckers
can help us fix that,

00:16:47.286 --> 00:16:48.987
so let's go get them.

00:16:48.987 --> 00:16:51.528
You and your friends doing
that shit when you were kids.

00:16:51.528 --> 00:16:53.425
Let's go get'm, let's go get'm.

00:16:53.425 --> 00:16:55.196
And then you shoot the motherfuckers

00:16:55.196 --> 00:16:57.177
and they can't say you missed
because they chest blow up

00:16:57.177 --> 00:16:59.521
and all that kind of shit
happens, and it's great.

00:16:59.521 --> 00:17:02.038
Because I just happened
to do a British talk show,

00:17:02.038 --> 00:17:03.524
and this guy asked me if
there were any directors

00:17:03.524 --> 00:17:05.414
that I'd never worked with
that I wanted to work with,

00:17:05.414 --> 00:17:08.348
and I realized George was
about to start Star Wars,

00:17:08.348 --> 00:17:10.694
and I said I'd love to
work with George Lucas.

00:17:10.694 --> 00:17:12.802
(audience applauds)

00:17:12.802 --> 00:17:15.314
and somebody that worked for him told him

00:17:15.314 --> 00:17:17.043
because they were doing
pre production in London.

00:17:17.043 --> 00:17:19.921
I left there and I went to San Francisco,

00:17:19.921 --> 00:17:24.044
and I was doing Sphere out
in the Napa Valley somewhere.

00:17:24.044 --> 00:17:27.422
I got a call and they said
George wants to meet with you

00:17:27.422 --> 00:17:29.354
if you're really serious
about being in Star Wars.

00:17:29.354 --> 00:17:30.865
I was like, great.

00:17:30.865 --> 00:17:32.745
So they sent a car out.

00:17:32.745 --> 00:17:35.205
I went over to the ranch.
I met with him.

00:17:35.205 --> 00:17:38.462
He was like, you know, I've
seen a couple things you're in,

00:17:38.462 --> 00:17:39.985
but I don't really ...

00:17:39.985 --> 00:17:42.047
I'm like well you don't
have to know, it's okay,

00:17:42.047 --> 00:17:43.696
I just want to be in the movie.

00:17:43.696 --> 00:17:45.345
And he's like, well, I don't know.

00:17:45.345 --> 00:17:47.780
I'm like look, I'll be a storm
trooper (audience laughs).

00:17:47.780 --> 00:17:49.757
I just want to run across the screen.

00:17:49.757 --> 00:17:53.108
Nobody needs to know I'm in
this movie but me, you know.

00:17:53.108 --> 00:17:54.628
(audience applauds)

00:17:54.628 --> 00:17:56.897
I will run across the screen
with a white helmet on.

00:17:56.897 --> 00:17:58.727
I would have been happy

00:17:58.727 --> 00:18:01.223
because I just wanted to
be in a Star Wars movie.

00:18:01.223 --> 00:18:02.956
- You were a Jedi knight though.

00:18:02.956 --> 00:18:05.627
- Well, I had no idea, until they called.

00:18:05.627 --> 00:18:06.700
I left there.

00:18:06.700 --> 00:18:09.920
George says, well, you
know, I got a couple things.

00:18:09.920 --> 00:18:11.521
I got some palace guard stuff.

00:18:11.521 --> 00:18:13.770
Maybe we'll find something.

00:18:13.770 --> 00:18:15.561
I was like okay great.

00:18:15.561 --> 00:18:18.196
So I went off, and it was
like three months later.

00:18:18.196 --> 00:18:19.765
I was done with Sphere,

00:18:19.765 --> 00:18:21.328
and I was ready to do something else.

00:18:21.328 --> 00:18:22.855
I got a call that said George wants you

00:18:22.855 --> 00:18:24.628
to come to London, can
you come to London now?

00:18:24.628 --> 00:18:26.070
I said, sure.

00:18:26.070 --> 00:18:27.878
I get on a plane, I go to
London, I get to the studio,

00:18:27.878 --> 00:18:29.733
they take me to this dressing room.

00:18:29.733 --> 00:18:31.509
And mind you, I still
haven't seen a script.

00:18:31.509 --> 00:18:33.484
I haven't seen anything.

00:18:33.484 --> 00:18:35.674
- But you already were willing to do--

00:18:35.674 --> 00:18:37.007
- I didn't care.

00:18:38.108 --> 00:18:39.467
- Here's the thing.

00:18:39.467 --> 00:18:41.508
You collect like little figurines, right?

00:18:41.508 --> 00:18:42.341
- Yeah.

00:18:42.341 --> 00:18:44.809
- So is that what you were angling for?

00:18:44.809 --> 00:18:46.336
- No.

00:18:46.336 --> 00:18:48.028
- I want to be a Star Wars figurine?

00:18:48.028 --> 00:18:50.011
I just wanted to be in Star Wars.

00:18:50.011 --> 00:18:51.288
You don't understand.

00:18:51.288 --> 00:18:52.694
When I sat there and saw that
movie for the first time,

00:18:52.694 --> 00:18:54.340
I was sitting there saying to myself,

00:18:54.340 --> 00:18:57.316
how in the hell do you get
in something like this?

00:18:57.316 --> 00:18:59.228
What do you have to do?

00:18:59.228 --> 00:19:03.352
Another movie, like Die
Hard, my friend Reginald Vel

00:19:03.352 --> 00:19:06.169
is that cop that's
outside eating doughnuts.

00:19:06.169 --> 00:19:08.109
I go, how did Reggie get that job?

00:19:08.109 --> 00:19:10.333
(audience laughs)

00:19:10.333 --> 00:19:11.567
So there are certain things that you watch

00:19:11.567 --> 00:19:12.845
that you just want to do.

00:19:12.845 --> 00:19:15.238
So when I got there and
I was in my dressing room

00:19:15.238 --> 00:19:17.254
or what they said was my dressing room,

00:19:17.254 --> 00:19:19.068
they go well somebody will
be up here to help you

00:19:19.068 --> 00:19:21.417
put on your costume in a
minute, you're having a fitting.

00:19:21.417 --> 00:19:24.761
Cool, I wonder whose dressing room this is

00:19:24.761 --> 00:19:28.231
because I see some books and I see, wow,

00:19:28.231 --> 00:19:30.871
that looks like a Jedi robe.

00:19:30.871 --> 00:19:32.103
I must be in somebody's room.

00:19:32.103 --> 00:19:34.743
Then somebody came in and
gave me these three pages.

00:19:34.743 --> 00:19:36.635
They go, these are your lines.

00:19:36.635 --> 00:19:40.615
And I went, Mace Windu,
who in the hell is that?

00:19:40.615 --> 00:19:44.685
And it's just Mace Windu,
Yoda; Mace Windu, Yoda;

00:19:44.685 --> 00:19:46.118
Mace Windu, Yoda.

00:19:46.118 --> 00:19:48.406
I know I'm not Yoda (audience laughs).

00:19:48.406 --> 00:19:50.158
I must be Mace Windu.

00:19:50.158 --> 00:19:52.478
Still didn't know who this guy was.

00:19:52.478 --> 00:19:54.740
And then they came in
and I put on some boots

00:19:54.740 --> 00:19:56.344
and they started putting the stuff on me,

00:19:56.344 --> 00:19:58.318
and I'm like, so I'm a Jedi?

00:19:58.318 --> 00:20:00.876
And they're like, well, yeah.

00:20:00.876 --> 00:20:03.266
I'm like wow, and I'm having
a conversation with Yoda.

00:20:03.266 --> 00:20:07.099
So I guess I won't be
edited out of the movie.

00:20:08.751 --> 00:20:11.262
So I won't be just running across screen.

00:20:11.262 --> 00:20:13.236
And then the guy came
in with the (mumbles),

00:20:13.236 --> 00:20:15.959
and he's like pick your lightsaber handle.

00:20:15.959 --> 00:20:19.870
I'm like ... (audience applauds)

00:20:19.870 --> 00:20:23.203
I can't even tell you what I really did.

00:20:25.154 --> 00:20:27.404
I really just went like ...

00:20:28.626 --> 00:20:32.008
I was like oh my God, that one,

00:20:32.008 --> 00:20:34.925
so that's when I knew I was a Jedi.

00:20:36.263 --> 00:20:38.077
- Didn't you think there'd
be a little ceremony

00:20:38.077 --> 00:20:40.827
where you'd be ignited as a Jedi?

00:20:42.158 --> 00:20:44.098
- I didn't care what was
going to happen at that point.

00:20:44.098 --> 00:20:46.900
All I knew was I was a
Jedi, I had my lightsaber,

00:20:46.900 --> 00:20:50.191
they told me to go in
this room, I met Frank Oz

00:20:50.191 --> 00:20:52.754
and all these guys, because
Yoda was still a puppet then,

00:20:52.754 --> 00:20:54.695
so I had to rehearse with these guys

00:20:54.695 --> 00:20:57.175
for like hours and hours
while they did ears and eyes

00:20:57.175 --> 00:20:59.735
and Yoda stuff, and I was
very cool with all that,

00:20:59.735 --> 00:21:02.249
but I made up my mind
then, I was like okay, so,

00:21:02.249 --> 00:21:06.402
I just got to be cool and
not give George any grief,

00:21:06.402 --> 00:21:08.900
be quiet, stay out of the way,

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:11.483
and see how long I hang around.

00:21:13.084 --> 00:21:14.276
Don't let him kill me.

00:21:14.276 --> 00:21:16.561
Don't kill me.
Don't kill me.

00:21:16.561 --> 00:21:18.145
And that's what I did.

00:21:18.145 --> 00:21:20.905
In the first movie, I sat in
the chair and I was like ...

00:21:20.905 --> 00:21:22.469
Everybody was having lightsaber battles.

00:21:22.469 --> 00:21:25.912
I wanted to like ...
Let it go, let it go.

00:21:25.912 --> 00:21:27.997
(audience laughs)

00:21:27.997 --> 00:21:30.195
- I'm cool, I'm fine.
- He'll get to ya.

00:21:30.195 --> 00:21:32.173
And I made it through the first one.

00:21:32.173 --> 00:21:34.193
So the second one, they
actually sent me a script.

00:21:34.193 --> 00:21:36.746
And I'm reading it and I see, oh wow,

00:21:36.746 --> 00:21:39.863
there's a huge, huge
battle scene in this arena.

00:21:39.863 --> 00:21:40.986
And I'm in it.

00:21:40.986 --> 00:21:45.133
But it's like recalled Jedis
from all over the galaxy

00:21:45.133 --> 00:21:47.889
to fight in this arena, and I'm like,

00:21:47.889 --> 00:21:49.450
how in the hell am I going to find myself

00:21:49.450 --> 00:21:51.625
in all this big old fight.

00:21:51.625 --> 00:21:55.704
George, do you think I could
have like a purple lightsaber?

00:21:55.704 --> 00:21:57.866
(audience laughs)

00:21:57.866 --> 00:21:59.919
And he was like, no
Sam, they're either red

00:21:59.919 --> 00:22:01.350
or they're green.

00:22:01.350 --> 00:22:04.949
But I'm the second baddest
Jedi in the universe,

00:22:04.949 --> 00:22:07.665
and I've been around for awhile.

00:22:07.665 --> 00:22:09.562
- Purple is kind of in the middle.

00:22:09.562 --> 00:22:12.193
